Z||000002||stimpy||07-10-2000||06:28 AM||||Got me one! Found a "Logic Soundlabs" 440w at a pawnshop for $80. Kind of a "no-name" amp, but has adjustable low-pass frequency and a Bass-boost circuit of some type, and is 2 ohm stable. Really moves the Polk 10. <BR>Do have a question though, see if anyone has a good guess. Another feature of the amp is "line out" RCA jacks. Whazzup with that? Does this mean I can put another amp inline with it? <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||07-10-2000||10:33 AM||||stimpy, i think it lets you run fewer rcas in the car. they probaly "pass through" without getting any cross over info from the amp. you could for example use that amp to run your top end speakers and run an rca out to another amp to run your sub. or run your front left and right with two channels and bridge two channels for your sub, then pass through to a smaller amp to run your rear speakers. some thing like that.<P>sorry, looked at the 440 watt and assumed 4 channels. no assumming jokes please.<p>[This message has been edited by Philphine (edited 07-10-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||stimpy||07-10-2000||10:49 PM||||Ooooh, that would be SO COOL! I still have a 200w that I could run my fronts off of. How would I go about checkin if that indeed is the case without blowin stuff up? <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Homicidal||07-10-2000||10:54 PM||||Check the wattage of the front speakers.  If they can't handle that much Wattage, (stock speaker DEFINATELY won't), then don't do it.<P>You can always turn the gain down, but I suggest checking the wattage of the speakers first.||||reg||1
Z||000006||stimpy||07-10-2000||11:06 PM||||Appreciate the input Homicidal, but not what I meant. Let me rephrase th question. I want to make sure the RCA line out on my amp isn't carrying a boosted signal that would blow either amp up. <BR>My factory 4x10's hit the trashpile many moons ago, replaced with Jensen OE replacement style. They are very soon to be replaced w/ Pioneers or other high quality 4x10. <P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://gifs123.tripod.com/page15/renstimpy2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000007||olympic||07-10-2000||11:18 PM||||   Most line outs work like this.  When you have your crossover bypassed ie-runnig your amp full range, the line outs will put out a full range signal you can run to another amp.  When you are useing your low pass crossover, the line outs will put out a high pass signal(say 80hz and up) which is whatever signal the amp isn't using itself.<P>   These line outs are very useful in multi amp systems or if your deck only has one RCA pair output.  Some people refer to this configuration as a "daisy chain".<P>  My CD player has 2 RCA output pairs.  I use one pair for my sub amp and the other for my mid/high amp.  This way I can fade between my  subs and mid/highs to balance the sound to my liking on-the-fly.  Otherwise I would have to constantly adjust my gains to change output levels.  Many newer decks come with 3 RCA output pairs for front, rear and sub.  This way you can fade your sound from front to back and still adjust your sub outut independantly.  I have an attenuator inline with my rear fill speakers to adjust their output.<P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||3
Z||000008||stimpy||07-10-2000||11:33 PM||||OK, heres where it gets tricky. The amp has a 3 position switch- full range, low pass, and high pass. Then it has 2 pots, for low pass x-over and high pass x-over. If I have the low pass switch on, and the x-over set at lowest bass setting, would the line out be an almost full range signal, at least enough to get some mid-bass from the dash? ||||reg||1
Z||000009||olympic||07-11-2000||01:10 AM||||   Set the low pass x-over between 70-90hz.   If you have small subs like 8" you can go even higher to 120-150 hz.  The lower 70-90hz setting will keep the bass output omni- directional  so it sounds like the bass is coming from the dash speakers.  There will be plenty of midbass for your front speakers.  4x10's probably cant even play frequencies below 80 hz anyway.<P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||1
